1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,120
This is a package registry for one team. Five
runs, three packages, one of them blocked.

2
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:23,380
pip install runs code before anyone has read
it. The build hook of a source package
executes with the installing user's rights: it
can read SSH keys, call out, leave files
behind. Everyone knows this, and everyone
installs packages nobody read — because
reading does not scale.

3
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:41,340
This is harborline 2.9.1. The model read it,
chose which probes to run, and executed them
in a container on Vultr with no network, a
read-only filesystem and no root. Verdict:
high. And the sentence that matters is the
first one — the package builds and imports
cleanly.

4
00:00:42,140 --> 00:01:01,080
Because the problem is not in the package. It
pins urllib3 1.24.1 — a release from 2018.
Thirteen known advisories, the worst scored
eight out of ten. The threshold is seven, so
this blocks. The wheels are fetched and
hashed, never installed. That hash is what you
would actually get.

5
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:16,620
Two runs earlier, the same package was fine.
2.8.0 and 2.9.0 both came back low — 2.9.0
with a note that one dependency was a minor
behind. The regression arrived with 2.9.1.

6
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:33,070
Every claim in this report points at a file:
the proxy log, the process result, the
filesystem diff. The model is allowed to
summarise them. It is not allowed to assert
anything they do not contain — a claim citing
a source that is not in this run gets dropped,
and you can see it here.

7
00:01:33,870 --> 00:01:41,690
That is the difference between a scanner and a
verdict: this one can show you why, on the
same screen, without you trusting a green
checkmark.

8
00:01:42,490 --> 00:01:48,010
Agent Rush. Source and MIT licence in the
repository, demo at this URL.
